  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 259 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nThrough the day today, a west CONUS ridge will be in the process\nof amplifying. As this occurs, weak shortwave energy will slide\nacross the Northern Plains. Simultaneously, surface low \npressure along the lee of the Rockies from Alberta through \neastern Montana and into western South Dakota will gradually \norganize over South Dakota. While there isn't a ton of moisture\nto work with, hi-res models and model forecast soundings do \nimply that light rain will track from west to east across the \nstate today, with all but the far southwest seeing low to medium\nchances for light rain. In regard to model forecast soundings, \nthey are in agreement that there is a fair amount of dry air to \novercome near the surface. However, it seems most of them \nsaturate enough to where light rain should be observed wherever \nreturns are able to organize for an extended period. The highest\nconfidence is in the north central and especially in the\nvicinity of the Turtle Mountains where saturation will be \neasier and there is around a 30 to 40 percent chance of a \nwetting rain (wetting rains are unlikely elsewhere).\n\nBeyond today, the dry pattern looks to continue. The\naforementioned ridge will significantly amplify over the High\nPlains resulting in flow that's north northwesterly over the\nstate. This will result in the common pattern where part of the\nstate remains on the warmer side of the ridge, and part of it on\nthe cooler side. As such, Wednesday through the remainder of the\nweek are favored to see seasonable (and on the cool side at\ntimes) temperatures east to above average temperatures west. The\nlatest trends have continued to push the cooler side slightly\nfurther west during the end of the workweek. Bottom line is \nthat high temperatures will range from the 30s and low 40s east\nto the 50s west. By this weekend, the ridge will begin sliding \neast over the Northern Plains as it deamplifies. This \ndeamplification will still allow for above average temperatures\nacross the entire state to start next week, though should also \nlimit their ceiling thus preventing anything extremely unusual \nfor this time of year.\n\nOf note, towards the end of the forecast period and beyond,\nthere are hints of a pattern change. Although disagreement\nin detailed evolution remains, models are fairly consistent in\nsome form of west CONUS trough developing early to mid next\nweek. Should this occur, more seasonable temperatures could\nreturn even across western parts of the state later next week, \nas well as potential precipitation chances.",
